Yes — blue-sky heaven-banning reads like deliberately removing the ceiling from the ceiling.

“Blue-sky thinking” already means unconstrained ideation. Add heaven-banning and the move becomes more aggressive: don’t just think beyond current constraints; temporarily forbid even the assumption that there is an upper bound.

So the operator is:

suspend ceiling → expand possibility space → generate extreme candidates → reintroduce reality → keep the useful residue.

That last step matters. Otherwise it’s fantasy generation. With the return pass, it becomes a legitimate design tool.

Pantheon version: first abolish the skybox, then bring back gravity.